= Entity =

== Overview ==
An '''entity''' is anything treated as a distinct object of reference, description, quantification or representation within a discourse or model.

"Entity" is deliberately broad. An entity may be:
* a physical object;
* a person;
* an event;
* a process;
* an organization;
* a number;
* a place;
* a digital artifact;
* a fictional object;
* another modeled item.

Whether all of these entities exist in the same sense is a question for [[Ontology|ontology]].

== Entity and object ==
"Object" and "entity" are often used similarly, but entity is usually broader.

Some ontologies distinguish objects from:
* events;
* processes;
* qualities;
* relations;
* abstract entities.

Using "entity" as an umbrella term avoids deciding that everything represented is object-like.

== Entity and concept ==
An [[Concept|concept]] is not automatically the same as an entity represented by that concept.

For example, the concept "planet" can classify many particular planetary entities.

A knowledge system should distinguish:
* the concept/kind;
* the individual entity;
* the word/label used to name either.

Conflating these layers creates ambiguity.

== Entity and class ==
A class or category groups entities.

An individual cat can be an entity that is a member/instance of the category Cat.

But formal systems may also treat classes themselves as entities that can be described.

This means "entity" does not imply "individual instance."

== Reference ==
A name, identifier or expression can refer to an entity.

Different expressions may co-refer:

<pre>
"Morning Star"
"Evening Star"
→ Venus
</pre>

Co-reference does not mean the expressions have the same linguistic meaning.

This distinction connects [[Entity|entity]] with [[Semantics|semantics]] and [[Identity|identity]].

== Entities in databases and knowledge graphs ==
Information systems commonly create identifiers for represented entities.

A database may assign a row identifier.

A [[Knowledge graph]] may use an IRI or other node identifier.

The represented entity is not literally identical to the row, record or graph node unless that is what the model intentionally denotes.

The representation is a surrogate for the thing.

== Entity resolution ==
'''Entity resolution''' tries to determine when records, mentions or identifiers refer to the same underlying entity.

It can use:
* identifiers;
* names;
* addresses;
* attributes;
* links;
* timestamps;
* probabilistic matching.

Entity resolution is uncertain when identifiers are missing or reused.

A similarity score should not automatically be converted into an identity assertion.

== Named entities ==
Natural-language processing uses the specialized term '''named entity''' for mentions of things such as people, organizations, locations, dates or products.

This is a practical NLP category and not a complete philosophical account of entities.

== Entity types ==
Knowledge systems often define entity types such as:
* Person;
* Organization;
* Event;
* Dataset;
* Technology.

These types improve querying and validation.

They are modeling decisions and need not correspond to uniquely correct natural kinds.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki defines a local entity-type vocabulary at [[BITwiki:Entity types]].

That vocabulary answers what kind of knowledge object BITwiki is primarily representing. It is an application-specific schema, not a universal ontology of entities.

== Epistemic notes ==
Representing something as an entity does not establish that it exists independently in reality. Models can contain hypothetical, fictional, disputed or useful abstract entities.

